GPU-side next implementation plan
(previous items here — pinned memory, TF32, fused AdamW, vocab padding — are all
applied in train.py/model_kv.py now, so this file has been reset to what's actually next.)

1. Parallel/prefetching data loading
Your ShardedDataset (dataloader2.py) is fully synchronous: next_batch() reads and
slices a memmapped shard on the main thread, so the GPU sits idle every time a new
batch is fetched. Right now shards are pre-tokenized .npy files, so the per-batch
cost is just disk read + slicing, not tokenization — but on a network-backed or
slow disk (common on free-tier Kaggle/Colab), that read can still stall the GPU.

Fix: wrap ShardedDataset's shard-reading logic in a real torch.utils.data.Dataset
(or IterableDataset) and drive it through torch.utils.data.DataLoader with
num_workers > 0. That spins up background worker processes to prefetch and hand
off the next batch while the GPU is still working on the current one, instead of
the strict fetch-then-compute serial pattern you have now.

2. Gradient checkpointing
Free-tier GPUs (T4/P100 per RESOURCES for the free-GPU platforms) have limited
VRAM. Gradient checkpointing trades compute for memory: instead of storing every
layer's activations for the backward pass, it recomputes them on the fly, cutting
activation memory at the cost of a second forward pass per checkpointed segment.
Worth adding if/when you hit OOM trying to increase batch size or move to a larger
GPT-2 variant (gpt2-medium/large) on a free-tier card.
Reference: torch.utils.checkpoint.checkpoint — wrap each transformer Block's
forward call.

3. torch.compile mode tuning
train.py already calls torch.compile(model) with the default mode. PyTorch also
offers mode="max-autotune", which spends more compile time searching for faster
kernels — worth an A/B timing comparison once the above two items are in, since
compile time itself eats into a limited free-tier GPU-hour budget and needs to be
weighed against the runtime speedup.
